{:trait/id :trait-ind-family-collectivism, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Extended family & clan collectivism prioritized over individual goals", :trait/description "Decision-making in India often involves extended family consensus. Marriages, career choices, migrations consult multi-generational households. Business partnerships value family trust.", :trait/contrast "NOT anti-individual; Indians pursue ambition within family/caste frameworks, not against them.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Shweder et al. (2006) 'Life History of Shame in South Asia'; Kakar (1978) 'Indian Psychoanalysis'; Hofstede & Hofstede (2010)", :trait/caveat "Urban, educated, younger-generation Indians diverge significantly. Caste-based hierarchy intersects family structure (Dalit, OBC experiences differ from upper-caste norms). Diaspora & immigrant-generation dynamics add complexity. Individual variation is substantial."}
{:trait/id :trait-ind-time-flexible, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Time as fluid, relationship-centered (polychronic); punctuality flexible", :trait/description "Indian time culture prioritizes relationships and task completion over clock time. Meetings start late, multiple activities happen concurrently. Deadline flexibility is normal. Spiritual/cyclical time also influences business rhythms.", :trait/contrast "NOT irresponsibility; reflects that human relationships and adaptive flexibility are valued over rigid schedules.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Edward T. Hall 'Beyond Culture'; Hofstede IVR (Indulgence); Bhaskaran (2011) 'Business Culture in India'", :trait/caveat "Corporate/multinational sectors adopt Western monochronic norms. IT industry (Bangalore, Mumbai) operates under different time culture than traditional sectors. Regional variation (North vs South). Generational shifts occurring."}
{:trait/id :trait-ind-hierarchy-respect, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Hierarchical respect, deference to elders & authority", :trait/description "Indian culture emphasizes respect for hierarchy: parents, elders, teachers, bosses command deference. Status & rank are important. Power distance (Hofstede term) is high. Addressing superiors informally is disrespectful.", :trait/contrast "Not blind obedience; respect is contingent on reciprocal care/duty (dharma) from hierarchy.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede Power Distance dimension; Kakar 'Indian Psychoanalysis'; Shweder et al.", :trait/caveat "Caste system historically enforced hierarchy; post-independence erosion ongoing. Younger urban professionals challenge traditional hierarchy. Gender hierarchies intersect (women's autonomy varies by caste, class, region). Dalit communities actively resist imposed hierarchy."}
{:trait/id :trait-ind-spiritual-pragmatism, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Spirituality & pragmatism coexist; fatalism mixed with agency", :trait/description "Indian worldview integrates spiritual/fatalistic (karma, dharma) with practical pragmatism. Individual effort & destiny both matter. Religion permeates everyday decisions (auspicious days, astrology, rituals). Business decisions may reference spiritual guidance.", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; integrated worldview where material & spiritual are inseparable.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Kakar (1978); Marriott (1976) 'Hindu transactions'; Shweder & Bourne (1984)", :trait/caveat "Urban, secular, educated Indians increasingly distance from ritual-based decision-making. Hindu-majority framing; Muslim, Christian, Sikh, Buddhist minorities have different spiritual frameworks. Generational/class variation high. Diaspora often retains spiritual practices more strongly than home-country peers."}
{:trait/id :trait-tokyo-time-compression, :trait/city [:city/id "JPY-tokyo"], :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "JPN"], :trait/title "Time compression extreme: megacity tempo intensified", :trait/description "Tokyo's monochronic time culture reaches extremes. Train schedules precise to 15 seconds. Punctuality obsession intensified by urban density & crowding. Work culture extends time commitments (overtime, after-work socializing with bosses).", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural Hokkaido: seasonal time rhythm, agricultural calendar, slower pace.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Observation from Hall 'Beyond Culture'; Tobin (1992) 'Japanese Preschools'; Tokyo business anthropology", :trait/caveat "Generational splits: Gen-Z Tokyo residents increasingly question overtime culture. Remote work (post-COVID) is changing time rigidity. Urban variety exists: Shibuya vs Chiyoda vs Shinjuku have different time paces."}
{:trait/id :trait-tokyo-hierarchical-intensity, :trait/city [:city/id "JPY-tokyo"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "JPN"], :trait/title "Hierarchical deference intensified in corporate megacity", :trait/description "Tokyo corporate culture exhibits extreme hierarchical respect: rigid seating order by rank, formal bowing angles by status, after-work drinking (nomikai) as mandatory hierarchy-enforcement ritual. Age = rank. Challenging authority is seen as immature.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural/small-town Japan: more casual, less formal status distinction.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Kaplan & Ridley (2008) 'The Boomer Exodus'; Lebra (1976) 'Japanese Patterns of Behavior'", :trait/caveat "Younger Tokyo professionals (especially in creative/tech fields) challenge rigid hierarchy. Women's position in hierarchy historically marginal; changing but slowly. Non-Japanese workers in Tokyo often find hierarchy unnatural & alienating."}
{:trait/id :trait-mex-extended-family-migration-resilience, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "MEX"], :trait/title "Extended family as migration & economic resilience network (already in Phase 2, adding city contrast)", :trait/description "Mexico City: urban familiarity with migration; Guadalajara traditional; rural villages depend on remittance networks from USA. Family = economic survival unit.", :trait/contrast "NOT static; constantly adapting to migration pressures.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Mexican migration studies", :trait/caveat "Urban/rural remittance dependence differs. Class affects migration access (wealthy legal, poor undocumented risks)."}
{:trait/id :trait-cub-music-resistance-revolutionary-culture, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "CUB"], :trait/title "Music & performing arts as political resistance & cultural survival", :trait/description "Cuba: music (son, timba, rap) channels dissent. Carnival & performances covert protest spaces. Government controls narrative yet artists persist. Revolution discourse permeates expression.", :trait/contrast "NOT overt; coded, metaphorical resistance.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Cuban cultural studies", :trait/caveat "Post-1990 Special Period shaped music pessimism. Younger generation hip-hop more explicit. Censorship ongoing; artists self-censor or emigrate."}
{:trait/id :trait-dom-caribbean-warmth-inequality-stark, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "DOM"], :trait/title "Caribbean warmth coexisting with extreme inequality & violence", :trait/description "Dominican Republic: warm, expressive culture alongside stark inequality (Haitian migrants, gang violence, femicide). Tourism wealth isolated from majority poverty. Resilience through music/baseball.", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; warmth = survival in hardship.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Dominican Republic sociology", :trait/caveat "Santo Domingo cosmopolitan; rural agrarian. Gang violence shapes Dominican identity differently in different regions. Gender: femicide epidemic. Class deeply stratified."}
{:trait/id :trait-ken-ubuntu-english-colonial-legacy, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "KEN"], :trait/title "Ubuntu philosophy & English colonial administrative legacy coexistence", :trait/description "Kenya: Ubuntu (harambee = pulling together) central yet colonial bureaucracy remains. Nairobi cosmopolitan; rural tribally-oriented. Economic inequality stark. 2007-2008 post-election violence trauma.", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; Kenyans navigate dual systems pragmatically.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Kenyan post-conflict studies", :trait/caveat "Nairobi vs rural vastly different. Tribal identity remains salient post-election trauma. Class affects colonial legacy experience (educated embrace it; rural resent it)."}
{:trait/id :trait-ugd-ubuntu-idi-amin-ptsd-resilience, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "UGA"], :trait/title "Ubuntu resilience amid Amin dictatorship & post-conflict trauma", :trait/description "Uganda (1971-1979 Amin genocide, 1980s-2005 civil war): Ubuntu communalism sustained people through atrocity. Yet collective trauma shapes baseline. Music, storytelling, faith = healing pathways.", :trait/contrast "NOT erasure; trauma real, resilience real.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede, Ugandan post-conflict psychology", :trait/caveat "Kampala educated vs rural subsistence vastly different. Genocide survivors (Acholi/Lango) vs perpetrators (Baganda) diverge in perspective. Intergenerational trauma ongoing. Class affects trauma access to therapy."}
{:trait/id :trait-zam-ubuntu-HIVAID-resilience-ubuntu, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "ZAM"], :trait/title "Ubuntu philosophy as HIV/AIDS collective caregiving model", :trait/description "Zambia (HIV/AIDS 11.5% prevalence): Ubuntu extended to care for sick/orphaned. Grandmothers raise grandchildren; compounds share responsibility. Yet biomedical Western treatment marginalizes traditional approaches.", :trait/contrast "NOT absence of modern medicine; pragmatic blend.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede, African public health", :trait/caveat "Urban Lusaka more Western-medicine-oriented; rural Ubuntu-traditional. Gender: women disproportionately care. Class affects treatment access (wealthy antiretroviral, poor generic/none)."}
{:trait/id :trait-tokyo-tech-forward, :trait/city [:city/id "JPY-tokyo"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "JPN"], :trait/title "Tech adoption as identity, innovation as cultural value", :trait/description "Tokyo's identity is intertwined with technological modernity. Early adoption of new tech (phones, gadgets, AI) is aspirational. Tech companies (SoftBank, Rakuten, startups) shape cultural narratives. Failure to adopt new tech is seen as backward.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural Japan: tech adoption slower, traditional values retained longer.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Observation; Yamamura & Saxonhouse (1989) 'Technology, Institutions & Economic Growth'", :trait/caveat "Tokyo has pockets of tech skepticism & traditional crafts pride (traditional textiles, tea ceremony). Rural tech adoption gap is shrinking. Generational: older Tokyo residents may resist smartphone culture."}
{:trait/id :trait-vnm-polychronic-time-relational, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "VNM"], :trait/title "Polychronic time, relationships prioritized over schedules", :trait/description "Vietnamese culture values relationships & flexibility over rigid punctuality. Time is event-based (when work finishes) rather than clock-based. Meetings may start late; priorities shift based on relationships & hierarchy. Colonial French & Buddhist influence blend with pragmatic communist adaptation.", :trait/contrast "NOT absence of time consciousness; rather, people-centered time prioritization.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede Cultural Dimensions (polychronic time); Myrsiades (1988) 'Vietnam: Cultural Encounters'", :trait/caveat "Urban Hanoi/Ho Chi Minh business culture increasingly monochronic due to globalization. Younger Vietnamese adopt clock time. Generational divide: older respect event-time, younger align with international business norms. Class matters: wealthy professionals adopt monochronic; rural agricultural villages retain event-time."}
{:trait/id :trait-pak-family-honor-patriarchal, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "PAK"], :trait/title "Family honor & patriarchal authority, shame-based discipline", :trait/description "Pakistani culture (majorly Muslim) centers family honor & male authority. Individual reputation reflects on entire family. Shame (not guilt) is the moral brake. Collective decisions (marriage, career) involve patriarchal approval. Diaspora maintains honor-shame even in Western contexts. Women's autonomy constrained by honor concerns.", :trait/contrast "NOT absence of female agency; many Pakistani women navigate constraints strategically.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede Power Distance; Kakar 'Indian Psychoanalysis' (shared South Asian frameworks); anthropology of Pakistani honor cultures", :trait/caveat "Urban educated Muslims increasingly question patriarchal norms. Honor-shame strongest in rural/conservative contexts. Ethnic diversity (Punjabi/Sindhi/Pashtun/Baloch) shows variation. Diaspora Pakistani youth (UK, USA, Canada) often negotiate between honor-shame & individualism. LGBTQ+ Pakistanis face severe honor-based rejection."}
{:trait/id :trait-bgd-family-kinship-cyclone-resilience, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "BGD"], :trait/title "Family kinship networks as cyclone & economic resilience, collective vulnerability management", :trait/description "Bangladesh (climate-vulnerable, dense population, frequent cyclones) relies on extended family networks for survival. Kinship bonds activate during disasters. Economic precarity drives collectivism. Remittances from diaspora (USA, UAE, UK) support villages. Interdependence is not cultural preference but survival necessity.", :trait/contrast "NOT sentimental; pragmatic kinship economics.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede Collectivism; Bangladesh climate-resilience studies", :trait/caveat "Urban Dhaka middle-class increasingly nuclearize. Climate migration & diaspora remittance dependency are relatively new (last 30 years); older narratives don't capture this shift. Gender: women disproportionately manage kinship labor. Rural/urban divide stark."}
{:trait/id :trait-phl-spanish-colonial-legacy-catholic-relational, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "PHL"], :trait/title "Spanish colonial religious legacy, Catholic emphasis on personal relationships & harmony", :trait/description "Philippines (350 years Spanish colonial rule, 80%+ Catholic) retains Spanish cultural imprint: emphasis on personal relationships (pakikipagkapwa-tao), indirect communication to preserve harmony, deference to authority figures, religious observance. Family business model common. Mañana attitude (flexible time).", :trait/contrast "NOT indolence; rather, relationship-prioritized pace.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede Cultural Dimensions; Enriquez 'Filipino Psychology' (indigenous psychology framework)", :trait/caveat "Metro Manila cosmopolitan vs provincial rural vastly different. Gen-Z Manila adopts global norms faster. Protestant minorities (11%) have different religious values. Chinese-Filipino business class blends Confucianism with Spanish Catholicism. Islamic Mindanao culturally distinct (not Spanish-influenced)."}
{:trait/id :trait-mmr-buddhist-acceptance-military-trauma-resilience, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "MMR"], :trait/title "Buddhist acceptance & non-violence ideals coexist with military dictatorship trauma & ethnic conflict", :trait/description "Myanmar (Theravada Buddhist majority, 1962-2011 military dictatorship, ongoing Rohingya crisis & ethnic conflicts) embodies contradiction: Buddhist non-violence coexists with state violence trauma & ethnic tensions. Spirituality as escape from political suffering. Intergenerational trauma from dictatorship & current civil war (2021-present).", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; Buddhists navigate coexistence with grim political reality.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede Power Distance (high, reflecting dictatorship legacy); Myanmar post-conflict psychology", :trait/caveat "Yangon urban vs rural ethnic areas vastly different. Ethnic diversity (Burman/Shan/Rakhine/Kachin) shapes political trauma differently. Rohingya Muslim minority face existential threat (not trauma, but genocidal conditions). Gen-Z exposed to 2021 coup differently than older generations. LGBTQ+ persecution ongoing (colonially-inherited laws)."}
{:trait/id :trait-lao-polychronic-buddhist-french-colonial-blend, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "LAO"], :trait/title "Polychronic Buddhist time, French colonial administrative legacy, relaxed pace (Lao Lao philosophy)", :trait/description "Laos (Buddhist, French colonial history, communist government 1975-present) blends polychronic time with Buddhist philosophy (acceptance, non-striving). 'Bab Pen Yang' (that's how it is) philosophy reflects fatalistic pragmatism. French influenced bureaucracy & French language (educated class). Slower pace than regional neighbors (Vietnam, Thailand).", :trait/contrast "NOT passivity; rather, acceptance-based pragmatism.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede polychronic time; Hall 'Beyond Culture'; Southeast Asia comparative anthropology", :trait/caveat "Vientiane capital more influenced by Thai/Western globalization than rural areas. Educational elite (French-educated, Thai-educated) have different time orientation. Ethnic Lao vs highland ethnic minorities differ culturally. Remote villages retain stronger Buddhist time orientation than urban centers."}
{:trait/id :trait-mys-ethnic-diversity-harmony-seeking, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "MYS"], :trait/title "Ethnic diversity (Malay/Chinese/Indian), harmony-seeking despite tensions", :trait/description "Malaysia (60% Malay Muslim, 25% Chinese, 8% Indian) maintains delicate ethnic balance through constitutional protections & shared commerce. 'Rukunegara' (principles of unity) emphasize harmony over explicit conflict. Hierarchical respect for rulers. Business is multicultural; private spaces are ethnically segregated.", :trait/contrast "NOT absence of tension; carefully managed beneath surface.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede; Embong (2010) 'State in Southeast Asia'; Malaysian studies", :trait/caveat "Urban Kuala Lumpur cosmopolitan; rural areas more ethnically homogeneous. Younger generation questions ethnic quotas. LGBTQ+ face legal persecution (sodomy laws inherited). Polarization increasing post-2018."}
{:trait/id :trait-lka-buddhist-conflict-resilience-caste-legacy, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "LKA"], :trait/title "Buddhist acceptance amid 26-year civil war trauma & Hindu/Muslim minority tensions", :trait/description "Sri Lanka (70% Sinhalese Buddhist, 15% Tamil Hindu) endured civil war (1983-2009). Buddhism emphasizes acceptance & non-violence; yet political violence uses Buddhist nationalism. Post-war trauma shapes baseline anxiety. Caste systems (Hindu Tamil & Muslim communities) intersect with ethnic conflict.", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; trauma coexists with spiritual practice.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede; post-conflict psychology studies; Raja (2012) 'Buddhist nationalism'", :trait/caveat "Colombo educated urban vs rural war zones vastly different. Tamil/Muslim minorities fear renewed violence. Intergenerational trauma. LGBTQ+ not criminalized but socially taboo. Religious extremism rising."}
{:trait/id :trait-sau-islamic-hierarchy-wahhabism-modernization-tension, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "SAU"], :trait/title "Islamic (Wahhabi) conservatism coexisting with rapid modernization pressures", :trait/description "Saudi Arabia (strict Islamic law, Wahhabi majority) balances religious hierarchy with Vision 2030 modernization. Women gaining rights (driving 2018+) yet remain legally subordinate to guardians. Youth vs elders have different modernity trajectories. Oil wealth enables rapid infrastructure change; culture lags.", :trait/contrast "NOT hypocrisy; genuine tension between tradition & modernization goals.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede High Power Distance; Kingdom Vision 2030; Saudi anthropology", :trait/caveat "Urban Riyadh/Jeddah significantly more liberal than rural Nejd. Younger Saudis (esp women, urban) challenge norms. Expatriate workers (30% population) have separate cultural norms. Shia minority face discrimination. LGBTQ+ criminalized; executions rare but social persecution severe."}
{:trait/id :trait-irn-persian-pride-revolution-legacy-islamic-authority, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IRN"], :trait/title "Persian cultural pride, revolutionary idealism, Islamic Republic authority hierarchy", :trait/description "Iran (Persian majority, Shia Islamic Republic since 1979) balances historical pride (Persian Empire legacy) with revolutionary ideology & religious rule. Hierarchy is religious (Supreme Leader) not just social. Young Iranians increasingly secular; older generations more revolutionary. Sanctions create collective economic anxiety.", :trait/contrast "NOT oppression monolith; complex internal resistance & conformity mix.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede; Iran studies; Azadeh (2011) 'Gendered State'; contemporary Iran anthropology", :trait/caveat "Tehran educated secular vs provincial conservative vastly different. Women's movement active but legally restricted (hijab mandatory). LGBTQ+ criminalized (execution possible). Minorities (Kurds, Baloch, Baha'i) face systemic discrimination. Diaspora retains cultural identity intensely."}
{:trait/id :trait-tur-ottoman-legacy-secularism-islamic-resurgence-tension, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "TUR"], :trait/title "Ottoman bureaucratic legacy, Atatürk secularism clashing with Islamic resurgence", :trait/description "Turkey (Muslim majority, secular republic 1923-) navigates Kemalist secularism vs Islamic political revival. Istanbul cosmopolitan & multicultural (Greek, Armenian, Jewish minorities historic). Anatolian heartland more conservative & religious. Honor culture in rural areas; gender roles evolving in cities.", :trait/contrast "NOT simple secular-vs-religious; layered historical tensions.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede; Keyman (2010) 'Turkish Politics'; Çağaptay (2006) 'Islam, Secularism & Nationalism'", :trait/caveat "Istanbul vs Anatolia divide deep. Kurdish population (20%) face marginalization. LGBTQ+ not criminalized but socially taboo & legally unprotected. Armenian & Greek minorities historically significant but now tiny diaspora. Political polarization intensifying."}
{:trait/id :trait-isr-jewish-diaspora-return-palestinian-conflict-coexistence-attempt, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "ISR"], :trait/title "Jewish diaspora return narrative, Palestinian conflict, attempted coexistence tensions", :trait/description "Israel (Jewish majority 74%, Arab minority 21%, Druze/others 5%) embodies diaspora return mythology & ongoing Palestinian conflict. Security concerns shape hypervigilance baseline. Kibbutz collectivism legacy; now mostly individualist startup culture. Arab Israelis face discrimination despite citizenship. Occupied territories create moral fractures.", :trait/contrast "NOT simple conflict; lived complexity of coexistence attempts.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede; Barth (2013) 'Israeli Identity & the Israeli-Palestinian Conflict'; ethnographic studies", :trait/caveat "Tel Aviv cosmopolitan vs Haredi religious communities vastly different. Ashkenazi/Sephardi/Mizrahi Jewish ethnic stratifications. Arab Israelis marginalized in economy & politics. Palestinians (West Bank/Gaza) experience apartheid conditions. LGBTQ+ legal rights in Israel, but Palestinian LGBTQ+ face family/honor violence. Settlers on West Bank separate ideologically from mainstream Israeli society."}
